# ── New install ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# You don't need to fill in anything below to get started. On first boot,
# fastPiHole generates on its own: a default dashboard password
# ("fastpihole", visible in the server log the first time — change it in
# Settings), a random session secret, and leaves the Pi-hole host at
# "pi.hole:443". The Pi-hole API token stays unconfigured until you add it
# yourself from Settings (the dashboard will warn you while it's missing).
# All of this lives encrypted/hashed in data/app.json, not here — a plain
# JSON file, not a database, so npm install doesn't need to compile
# anything native (relevant on ARM boards like a Raspberry Pi, where that
# can take a long time).
DASHBOARD_PORT=20053
NODE_ENV=production

# ── Upgrading from a previous install with .env ─────────────────────────
# Only read ONCE, on the first boot after updating, to import your
# existing configuration into data/app.json (and only if these aren't
# left as placeholders). After that migration they're ignored entirely:
# everything is edited from Settings from then on. You can delete these
# lines once you've confirmed the dashboard still works the same.
# PI_HOLE_HOST=pi.hole
# PI_HOLE_PORT=443
# PI_HOLE_API_TOKEN=your-application-password-here
# DASHBOARD_PASSWORD=your-secure-password-here
# SESSION_SECRET=your-random-session-secret-here

# ── Optional, any install ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Move the encryption master key or the settings file outside of data/
# (for example onto a separate encrypted volume). Both fall back to
# data/ (already in .gitignore) if left unset.
# MASTER_KEY_FILE=/etc/fast-pihole/master.key
# APP_DB_FILE=/etc/fast-pihole/app.json
